A||||8||Alex4mula||Which are the causes of low oil pressure?||||1||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||09-07-2001||10:11 AM||||My oil pressure gage is almost in the red zone on idle with the engine hot. It never goes above 40psi at higher RPM. Still it runs strong (8.5s 0-60). There's a V8 in my future plans but I'm curious about this. TIA<P>88 Formula, auto, 118K miles||||reg||1
Z||000001||Butter||09-07-2001||11:05 AM||||If you have v8 plans for the future why waste time worrying about the oil pressure on the present engine.  Go for the v8.  I did and have not regretted it yet.||||reg||10
Z||000002||88formula||09-07-2001||11:13 AM||||Did you get that reading with a mechanical gauge or the factory electrical gauge? Most of the time low oil pressure is caused by worn engine bearings. Sometimes on high mileage engines the oil pump can be the cause. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||09-07-2001||11:19 AM||||Are you sure the sender is accurate?<BR>You might try using heavier oil.<BR>When mine is hot, it's just above the red zone (just under 1/4 scale) at idle. Goes to just above half scale at speed.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||09-07-2001||11:32 AM||||The sender is new. It went low when I changed the oil for first time (my new car) and used 5W30. I know that putting heavier oil will improve. Did it with my old 2.8 and worked ok. The only thing with that is that it gets worst gas milage. The V8 is in the near future. Just need an excuse for wife to approve  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Just for my understanding, how worn bearings cause low oil pressure? My knowledge of engine internals is not the best. Tks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||OcalaFiero||09-07-2001||11:37 AM||||That happened to me with my '86 2M4. The first time I changed the oil in it, I used 10w30, and my oil pressure dropped, and the car started stalling at idle. It turns out the fuel pump relay was bad, along with a bad oil pressure sending unit, and the thinner oil was causing too little oil pressure to allow the fuel pump to run at low engine speeds. (Turns out the previous owner was using 20w50) ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steve Normington||09-07-2001||11:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>Just for my understanding, how worn bearings cause low oil pressure? My knowledge of engine internals is not the best. Tks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>When the bearings are worn, there is more gap between the bearings and the crank. More gap means that more oil will flow through the bearings. It's similar to putting your thumb over a hose. More gap means more flow means less pressure. Less gap means less flow means more pressure.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||09-07-2001||03:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>The sender is new. It went low when I changed the oil for first time (my new car) and used 5W30. I know that putting heavier oil will improve. Did it with my old 2.8 and worked ok. The only thing with that is that it gets worst gas milage. The V8 is in the near future. Just need an excuse for wife to approve   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Just for my understanding, how worn bearings cause low oil pressure? My knowledge of engine internals is not the best. Tks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Did you get a GM sending unit?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||bad911||09-07-2001||08:59 PM||||In a warm climate there's no reason not to run heavier oil especially in a performance car and with some mileage on it. The only precaution is don't hit the key and drive away... let 'er warm-up and think about it for a few seconds. I'd try 20-50, stay with a top brand like Valvoline Pennzoil or Quaker State. If you can be extravagant go to synthetic... Mobil 1 or Pennzoil. ||||reg||1
